  "question": {
    "text": "From a lot of 10 items, which include 3 defective items, a sample of 5 items is drawn at random. Let the random variable $\\mathrm{X}$ denote the number of defective items in the sample. If the variance of $\\mathrm{X}$ is $\\sigma^2$, then $96 \\sigma^2$ is equal to ______",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "56",
  "solution": {
    "text": "$X=$ denotes number of defective\n$\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline \\mathrm{x} & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\\n\\hline \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{x}) & \\frac{7}{15} & \\frac{5}{12} & \\frac{5}{12} & \\frac{1}{12} \\\\\n\\hline \\mathrm{x}_1{ }^2 & 0 & 1 & 4 & 9 \\\\\n\\hline \\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{i}} \\mathrm{x}_1{ }^2 & 0 & \\frac{5}{12} & \\frac{20}{12} & \\frac{9}{12} \\\\\n\\hline \\mathrm{p}_{\\mathrm{i}} \\mathrm{x}_{\\mathrm{i}} & 0 & \\frac{5}{12} & \\frac{10}{12} & \\frac{3}{12} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}$\n$\\mu=\\Sigma \\mathrm{p}_{\\mathrm{i}} \\mathrm{x}_{\\mathrm{i}}=\\frac{18}{12}$\n$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\Sigma \\mathrm{p}_{\\mathrm{i}} \\mathrm{x}_1^2=\\frac{34}{12} \\\\\n& \\sigma^2=\\Sigma \\mathrm{p}_{\\mathrm{i}} \\mathrm{x}_1^2-(\\mu)^2\n\\end{aligned}$\n$\\begin{aligned} & =\\frac{34}{12}-\\left(\\frac{18}{12}\\right)^2=\\frac{17}{6}-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ & \\frac{34-27}{12}=\\frac{7}{12} \\\\ & 96 \\sigma^2=96 \\times \\frac{7}{12}=56\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
